        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Conditional flat-specialization identity for the smooth vacuum Maxwell subsystem and the local Lorentz force/power density in the selected SI (-+++) dictionary. It does not assert that a nonzero field on the flat background solves the coupled Einstein equation, that curved spacetime has a global inertial chart, or that any adopted postulate is empirically true.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      "## Statement\n\nLet $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval, $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ a nonempty open region, and $U=I\\times\\Omega$ a flat inertial Minkowski model with metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, orientation $dx^0\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3$, and coordinates $x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ in metres. Let $E,B:U\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be jointly $C^1$, with every additional derivative displayed below existing and continuous, and let $\\rho,J$ be continuous. Use $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$, $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, $F^{0i}=E_i/c$, $F^{i0}=-E_i/c$, $F^{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, $j^0=c\\rho$, $j^i=J^i$, and $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ as fixed by [[def-emg-geometry-units]].\n\nOn this flat specialization, the tensor systems from the smooth curved postulate are\n\n$$(\\mathrm{TM1})\\quad \\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu}=-\\mu_0j^\\nu\\quad(\\nu=0,1,2,3),\\qquad (\\mathrm{TM2})\\quad \\partial_\\lambda F_{\\mu\\nu}+\\partial_\\mu F_{\\nu\\lambda}+\\partial_\\nu F_{\\lambda\\mu}=0\\quad(\\lambda,\\mu,\\nu=0,1,2,3).$$\n\nThey hold if and only if the four adopted smooth SI vacuum Maxwell equations hold on $U$:\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}B=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J+c^{-2}\\partial_tE.$$\n\nHere $\\partial_t$ is at fixed spatial position and $\\operatorname{div}$ and $\\operatorname{curl}$ use the positively oriented Euclidean spatial basis. In addition, for the tensor force density $f^\\nu:=F^{\\nu\\lambda}j_\\lambda$, its time and spatial components satisfy\n\n$$f^0=(J\\cdot E)/c,\\qquad f^i=\\rho E_i+(J\\times B)_i.$$\n\nThus they reproduce the adopted power density $J\\cdot E$ divided by $c$ and force density $\\rho E+J\\times B$ of [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]]. The tensor systems have exactly eight independent scalar equations: four sourced components and four independent homogeneous components. All these are conditional component identities for the selected smooth model; no empirical premise is used. The result checks only the Maxwell subsystem on a flat background and the local force dictionary, not the coupled Einstein equation."
